
    tclpdf - PDF generation for Tcl


    This directory contains a freely distributable pure tcl extension called tclpdf.


SYNOPSIS

    Create PDF documents from Tcl. tclpdf is pure Tcl built on TclOO - no
    compiler, no binary extension, no Tk. A document is an object, every
    command takes -options, and each topic module is loaded on first use.

      Graphics    paths, arcs, clipping, transparency, form XObjects, patterns,
                  shadings; grey, RGB, CMYK, spot, DeviceN, Lab, ICC colour
      Text        paragraphs, pagination and columns, text around shapes and
                  along a path, hyphenation, right-to-left, vertical writing
      Runs        bold, italic, links, headings and lists in flowing text,
                  runs in table cells - as a run list, as tags or Markdown
      Fonts       standard 14, TrueType, OpenType/CFF, Type 1, collections,
                  variable, Type 3, colour fonts and emoji, fallback chains
      Images      JPEG, PNG and TIFF, passed through where possible
      Tables      column widths, page breaks with repeated header and footer
                  rows, rowSpan/colSpan
      SVG         drawn as vectors, clip paths and masks; barcodes via tzint
      Navigation  links, bookmarks, page labels, attachments, annotations,
                  form fields, layers
      Existing    a page of another PDF placed as a form, a finished file
      PDFs        read or continued by incremental update
      Standards   tagged PDF, PDF/A-2 and -3 (B, U, A), PDF/UA-1 and -2,
                  ZUGFeRD / Factur-X, Order-X
      Security    AES-256 encryption, digital signatures, RFC 3161 timestamps


DEPENDENCIES

    Tcl >= 8.6.11, Tcl 9 as well. The core needs nothing else; Tk is never
    loaded. Optional:

      tdom 0.9.0+  every document claiming PDF/A, PDF/UA or ZUGFeRD (XMP);
                   SVG parses with it when present, with its own parser if not
      http         part of Tcl; loaded only to request a document timestamp
      tls          only for an https timestamp authority (or use -tsa)

    tzint is needed by your script for barcodes, not by tclpdf. The signature
    value comes from the caller (-signer); encryption is pure Tcl.


INSTALLING

    The release archives tclpdf<version>.zip and .tar.gz hold the modules,
    pkgIndex.tcl and icc/ - nothing to build: extract onto the auto_path
    and "package require tclpdf". The source archive, like a checkout, adds
    tests, examples and the manual source; build it in the source directory:

        ./configure [--with-tcl=<dir of tclConfig.sh>] [--prefix=<tree>]
        make test
        make install        into <prefix>/lib/tclpdf<version>
        make examples       every example into examples/out


DOCUMENTATION

    doc/tclpdf.md   the manual - "make all" makes .n and .html (pandoc)
    doc/PLUGINS.md  extending tclpdf without touching its source
    examples/       scripts, each saying in its header what it shows
    Wiki            https://fossil.sowaswie.de/tclpdf - manual, finished
                    example PDFs, technical notes
    Bugs            https://fossil.sowaswie.de/tclpdf/ticket, as anonymous


LICENCE

    Copyright (C) 2026 Alexander Schoepe, Bochum, DE
    Contributors - special thanks: Ralf Fassel

    MIT - see license.terms. It covers all that is installed: the modules,
    pkgIndex.tcl and icc/, whose profiles end up inside PDF/A documents.
    sRGB.icc reads "no copyright, use freely", the basICColor CMYK and grey
    intents are zlib (LICENSE-ZLIB-bICC), sRGB2014.icc is under the ICC's
    terms (LICENSE-ICC.txt); see icc/README.md. Build-time data, not
    installed: the Adobe Glyph List (tools/agl, BSD licence in its header)
    and the Adobe core font metrics (examples/assets/fonts/adobe-afm, with
    Adobe's LICENSE.txt, which must stay with them).

    NO FONT IS PART OF THE PACKAGE. The faces under examples/assets/fonts
    serve the tests and examples; make install and the release archives
    contain none, only the source archive carries them. Each is third-party
    work under its own licence - DejaVu, Google Fonts faces such as Roboto,
    Arimo and Noto, OCR A/B, URW Core 35, Liberation - with the text beside
    it or in fonts/licenses/; examples/assets/README.md records origin, terms
    and embedding flag of every face. Whether a font may be embedded is a
    question for its own licence: tclpdf reports fsType, but does not refuse
    a restricted face.


